description:
Found by the s525q binder dual-run (PCL_BINDER_CHECK=log), cpan-tests/modules/Text-CSV-2.04/t/66_formula.t:74 and :170: `_block_captures_name` (via _sub_captures_name < _captured_in_subs < _promote_captured) answers 1 for `$csv` in `sub parse { my $f = shift; my @d; ok (my $csv = Text::CSV->new(...), "new $f"); for (@data) { $csv->parse($_); ... } }`; the binder (pad.c) answers 0: every `$csv` in the body (80, 81) binds the `my $csv` declared INSIDE the call's argument list at 76 -- a `my` nested in an expression is introduced at the next statement like any other (op.c newSTATEOP -> intro_my) and lasts to the sub's end.

The legacy capture scan does not see a declaration nested inside a call's parenthesised arguments, so it reads the body's uses as the FILE lexical `$csv` (line 15) and promotes it.

Probe (scratch/s525q/probes/C1.pl in the s525q worktree):

    my $c = "outer";
    sub p { my $f = shift; print(my $c = "inner$f"); print "\n"; for (1) { $c .= "+" } $c }
    print p(1), "\n", $c, "\n";
    my $r = p(2); print "$r $c\n";

perl 5.40.3 and base (main 81ef05e5) print the SAME output (inner1 / inner1+ / outer / inner2 / inner2+ outer): NO WRONG VALUE measured.  The cost is structural: the file lexical becomes a promoted cell `$c__file__0` (:why :captured) and the sub's metadata says `:captures ($c__file__0)`, which it does not -- an unneeded promotion (a cell instead of a plain lexical) and wrong capture facts for any consumer that trusts them.

Legacy bug, BENIGN today.  Belongs to 1c (the capture-gate + promoter port asks the binder's `captures`).  NOT fixed in s525q (inert batch).

ALSO (same session, the gate in log mode): Pl/t/first-run-01.t:126-128 Side.pm -- a FILE-level `open(my $l, ">>", "side.log")` and `sub import { open(my $l, ...) ... }`: the sub is reported to capture the file-level $l (block_captures 1, binder 0).  Same shape: the declaration nested in a call's argument list.
